## Approach

This form leverages React Hook Form for performant, flexible form handling. We'll build our form using the `<Field />` component, which gives you **complete flexibility over the markup and styling**.

- Uses React Hook Form's `useForm` hook for form state management.
- `<Controller />` component for controlled inputs.
- `<Field />` components for building accessible forms.
- Client-side validation using Zod with `zodResolver`.

## Architecture & Components

### Component Overview

**Core Components** (from `form.tsx`):

- `Form` - Form provider that wraps React Hook Form's FormProvider
- `FormField` - Wrapper for Controller that provides field context
- `FormFieldContext` - Context exported for Field components to access field name

**Field Components** (from `field.tsx`):

- `Field` - Field container with automatic error detection and `data-invalid` attribute
- `FieldLabel` - Label that auto-binds to field control via `htmlFor`
- `FieldControl` - Wrapper that auto-sets `id`, `aria-describedby`, and `aria-invalid` on controls
- `FieldDescription` - Description text with auto-linked `id` for aria-describedby
- `FieldError` - Error message that auto-retrieves and displays validation errors
- `useField` - Hook to access field context (id, error state, etc.)

**Layout Components** (from `field.tsx`):

- `FieldSet` - Fieldset container for grouping related fields
- `FieldLegend` - Legend for fieldset with `variant` prop (`label` or `legend`)
- `FieldGroup` - Container that provides automatic spacing between fields
- `FieldSeparator` - Visual separator between field groups
- `FieldContent` - Content wrapper for horizontal layouts (label + description on one side)
- `FieldTitle` - Title for fields in horizontal layouts (alternative to FieldLabel)

### Critical Rules

**Field Name Prop**:

- ❌ **NOT needed** - `Field` automatically inherits `name` from `FormFieldContext` when used inside `FormField`
- The field name flows from `FormField` → `FormFieldContext` → `Field` → `useField()`

**FieldGroup Usage**:

- ✅ **ALWAYS wrap form fields in FieldGroup** (replaces `space-y-` or `flex flex-col gap-` divs)
- Even for single fields, wrap in `FieldGroup` for consistent spacing
- FieldGroup provides automatic vertical spacing with proper gaps

**Horizontal Layouts** (Switch/Checkbox):

- Use `Field` with `orientation="horizontal"`
- Use `FieldContent` to group label and description on one side
- Control (Switch/Checkbox) appears on the other side

**FieldControl Behavior**:

- Automatically handles `aria-*` attributes
- `FieldError` shows/hides errors automatically
- `FieldLabel` includes support for info tooltips via `info` prop

**🚨 Item vs Field**:

- **NEVER wrap FormField with Item/ItemContent** - `FormField` contains `Field` which provides all layout/styling
- Use `Item` components for displaying content (lists, cards, non-form elements)
- Use `Field` components for form inputs

**🚨 FieldGroup vs ItemGroup**:

- **ALWAYS use FieldGroup above FormField** for form inputs
- Use `ItemGroup` for non-form content (lists, menus, etc.)

### Form Structure Patterns

**Pattern for all forms**:

```tsx
<Form {...form}>
  <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
    <Section>
      <FieldGroup>
        <FormField name="..." control={form.control} render={...} />
        <FormField name="..." control={form.control} render={...} />
      </FieldGroup>
    </Section>
  </form>
</Form>
```

**Pattern with mixed content** (form + non-form sections):

```tsx
<Form {...form}>
  <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
    {/* Form section */}
    <Section>
      <FieldGroup>
        <FormField name="..." control={form.control} render={...} />
      </FieldGroup>
    </Section>

    {/* Non-form content section */}
    <Section>
      <ItemGroup>
        <Item>Content here</Item>
      </ItemGroup>
    </Section>
  </form>
</Form>
```

## Anatomy

Here's a basic example of a form using the `<Controller />` component from React Hook Form and the `<Field />` component.

```tsx showLineNumbers {5-18}
<Controller
  name="title"
  control={form.control}
  render={({ field }) => (
    <Field>
      <FieldLabel>Bug Title</FieldLabel>
      <FieldControl>
        <Input
          {...field}
          placeholder="Login button not working on mobile"
          autoComplete="off"
        />
      </FieldControl>
      <FieldDescription>
        Provide a concise title for your bug report.
      </FieldDescription>
      <FieldError />
    </Field>
  )}
/>
```

**Key features of this implementation:**

- `Field` automatically detects validation errors from form context and sets `data-invalid`
- `FieldLabel` automatically binds to the field control with proper `htmlFor`
- `FieldControl` wraps form controls and automatically sets `id`, `aria-describedby`, and `aria-invalid`
- `FieldError` automatically retrieves and displays errors from form context, hiding itself when there are no errors

## FieldControl Component

The `<FieldControl />` component is a wrapper that uses the Slot pattern from Radix UI to automatically manage accessibility attributes for form controls.

### What it does automatically:

- Sets unique `id` for the control (used by FieldLabel's `htmlFor`)
- Manages `aria-describedby` to link descriptions and error messages
- Sets `aria-invalid` based on validation state
- Merges these props with your control's existing props

### When to use it:

Wrap `<FieldControl />` around form controls like:

- `<Input />`, `<Textarea />`
- `<SelectTrigger />` (not the whole Select)
- `<Checkbox />`, `<Switch />`
- `<RadioGroupItem />` (not the whole RadioGroup)

### Example:

```tsx showLineNumbers
<Field>
  <FieldLabel>Email</FieldLabel>
  <FieldControl>
    <Input type="email" {...field} />
  </FieldControl>
  <FieldError />
</Field>
```

The `<Input />` will automatically receive:

- `id="unique-field-id"`
- `aria-describedby="unique-field-id-field-description unique-field-id-field-message"`
- `aria-invalid={true}` (if there are validation errors)

### Note on Slot pattern:

`<FieldControl />` uses Radix UI's Slot component, which means it doesn't render an extra DOM element. It merges all props directly onto its child component.

## Form

### Create a form schema

We'll start by defining the shape of our form using a Zod schema

<Callout icon={<InfoIcon />}>
**Note:** This example uses `zod v3` for schema validation, but you can
replace it with any other Standard Schema validation library supported by
React Hook Form.
</Callout>

```tsx showLineNumbers title="form.tsx"
import * as z from "zod";

const formSchema = z.object({
  title: z
    .string()
    .min(5, "Bug title must be at least 5 characters.")
    .max(32, "Bug title must be at most 32 characters."),
  description: z
    .string()
    .min(20, "Description must be at least 20 characters.")
    .max(100, "Description must be at most 100 characters."),
});
```

### Setup the form

Next, we'll use the `useForm` hook from React Hook Form to create our form instance. We'll also add the Zod resolver to validate the form data.

```tsx showLineNumbers title="form.tsx" {17-23}
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import * as z from "zod";

const formSchema = z.object({
  title: z
    .string()
    .min(5, "Bug title must be at least 5 characters.")
    .max(32, "Bug title must be at most 32 characters."),
  description: z
    .string()
    .min(20, "Description must be at least 20 characters.")
    .max(100, "Description must be at most 100 characters."),
});

export function BugReportForm() {
  const form = useForm<z.infer<typeof formSchema>>({
    resolver: zodResolver(formSchema),
    defaultValues: {
      title: "",
      description: "",
    },
  });

  function onSubmit(data: z.infer<typeof formSchema>) {
    // Do something with the form values.
    console.log(data);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      {/* ... */}
      {/* Build the form here */}
      {/* ... */}
    </form>
  );
}
```

### Build the form

We can now build the form using the `<Controller />` component from React Hook Form and the `<Field />` component.

