### Done

That's it. You now have a fully accessible form with client-side validation.

When you submit the form, the `onSubmit` function will be called with the validated form data. If the form data is invalid, React Hook Form will display the errors next to each field.

## Validation

### Client-side Validation

React Hook Form validates your form data using the Zod schema. Define a schema and pass it to the `resolver` option of the `useForm` hook.

```tsx showLineNumbers title="example-form.tsx" {5-8,12}
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import * as z from "zod";

const formSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
  description: z.string().optional(),
});

export function ExampleForm() {
  const form = useForm<z.infer<typeof formSchema>>({
    resolver: zodResolver(formSchema),
    defaultValues: {
      title: "",
      description: "",
    },
  });
}
```

### Validation Modes

React Hook Form supports different validation modes.

```tsx showLineNumbers title="form.tsx" {3}
const form = useForm<z.infer<typeof formSchema>>({
  resolver: zodResolver(formSchema),
  mode: "onChange",
});
```

| Mode          | Description                                              |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| `"onChange"`  | Validation triggers on every change.                     |
| `"onBlur"`    | Validation triggers on blur.                             |
| `"onSubmit"`  | Validation triggers on submit (default).                 |
| `"onTouched"` | Validation triggers on first blur, then on every change. |
| `"all"`       | Validation triggers on blur and change.                  |

## Displaying Errors

The `<FieldError />` component automatically retrieves validation errors from the form context and displays them. It handles everything for you:

### Automatic behavior:

- **Error detection**: Automatically retrieves error from form context (no need to pass `fieldState.error`)
- **Auto-hide**: Hides itself when there are no errors (no need for conditional rendering)
- **Accessibility**: Properly linked via `aria-describedby` (handled by `FieldControl`)
- **Styling**: `Field` automatically adds `data-invalid={true}` when there are errors

### Basic usage:

```tsx showLineNumbers title="form.tsx" {5-6,10}
<Controller
  name="email"
  control={form.control}
  render={({ field }) => (
    <Field>
      <FieldLabel>Email</FieldLabel>
      <FieldControl>
        <Input {...field} type="email" />
      </FieldControl>
      <FieldError />
    </Field>
  )}
/>
```

### Custom error messages by type:

Use the `messages` prop to provide custom error messages for specific error types:

```tsx showLineNumbers title="form.tsx" {3-6}
<FieldError
  messages={{
    required: "Please enter your email address",
    invalid_string: "Please enter a valid email address",
  }}
/>
```

The error type comes from your Zod schema validation. If a custom message is provided for the error type, it will be used instead of the default error message.

